1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,875
Two-dimensional arrays.

2
00:00:01,875 --> 00:00:02,852
Plus the grid bug

3
00:00:02,852 --> 00:00:04,599
that catches everyone.

4
00:00:04,599 --> 00:00:06,777
Give NumPy a list of lists.

5
00:00:06,827 --> 00:00:09,005
grid.shape is two by three:

6
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:11,079
two rows, three columns.

7
00:00:11,129 --> 00:00:12,360
grid[1, 2] means

8
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:14,490
row one, column two.

9
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:16,128
grid[:, 1] takes

10
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:17,945
a whole column.

11
00:00:18,295 --> 00:00:19,286
Now the bug.

12
00:00:19,286 --> 00:00:20,060
Build a grid from

13
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:21,248
plain lists like this,

14
00:00:21,248 --> 00:00:22,969
then change one cell.

15
00:00:22,969 --> 00:00:24,710
Every row changed!

16
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:25,598
All three rows

17
00:00:25,598 --> 00:00:26,757
are the same list,

18
00:00:26,757 --> 00:00:27,809
so one change shows

19
00:00:27,809 --> 00:00:29,174
up everywhere.

20
00:00:29,324 --> 00:00:31,080
Use a comprehension instead,

21
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:32,045
so each row is a

22
00:00:32,045 --> 00:00:33,512
brand new list.

23
00:00:33,862 --> 00:00:34,687
Recap.

24
00:00:34,687 --> 00:00:36,893
grid[r, c] is one cell.

25
00:00:36,893 --> 00:00:39,188
grid[:, c] is a column.

26
00:00:39,188 --> 00:00:40,527
And build plain-list grids

27
00:00:40,527 --> 00:00:42,213
with a comprehension.

28
00:00:42,563 --> 00:00:44,010
Next up: tuples.

29
00:00:44,010 --> 00:00:45,946
Follow for the whole series.
